# Long-case expansion L5 (target ~25–30 Qs): leg ulcers, hidradenitis suppurativa, xeroderma pigmentosum.
# Own words; viva-length answers. Facts from IADVL 5e and standard references.
D = [
dict(case="Leg ulcers", qa=[
 ("Describe the arterial ulcer and peripheral arterial disease assessment.", ["Risk: smoking, diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidaemia, age; claudication → rest pain → tissue loss (Fontaine/ Rutherford stages)", "Ulcer: punched-out, deep, pale/ necrotic base, little granulation, on toes, heel, lateral malleolus, shin; cold, pale, hairless shiny skin, thickened nails, absent pulses, delayed capillary refill, Buerger's test (pallor on elevation, rubor on dependency)", "ABPI <0.9 PAD, <0.5 critical; toe pressure <30 mmHg poor healing; duplex/ CT angiography; refer for revascularisation; no compression if ABPI <0.5"]),
 ("Describe the diabetic foot ulcer and its classification.", ["Neuropathic (painless, plantar pressure points, callus), ischaemic, or neuro-ischaemic; infection and Charcot foot complicate", "Wagner grading: 0 at-risk foot, 1 superficial ulcer, 2 deep ulcer to tendon/ capsule, 3 deep with abscess/ osteomyelitis, 4 forefoot gangrene, 5 whole-foot gangrene; also University of Texas classification (grade + stage — infection/ ischaemia)", "Management: offloading (total contact cast), debridement, infection control (probe-to-bone test — osteomyelitis), glycaemic control, revascularisation, footwear education"]),
 ("What is the role of compression — types and pressure?", ["Elastic (long-stretch) bandages/ stockings — high resting pressure; inelastic (short-stretch) bandages/ Unna boot — high working pressure, low resting pressure (better tolerated, good with calf pump)", "Multilayer systems (4-layer: orthopaedic wool, crepe, elastic, cohesive) deliver ~40 mmHg at ankle; stockings — class I (14–17 mmHg), II (18–24, UK) / 20–30 (international), III (25–35) / 30–40 mmHg", "Contraindications: ABPI <0.5–0.6, severe heart failure, severe neuropathy (caution), acute DVT (relative), skin infection"]),
 ("Discuss dressings for leg ulcers.", ["Choose by exudate and wound bed: hydrogels (dry/ sloughy — autolytic debridement), hydrocolloids (low–moderate exudate), foams (moderate–heavy exudate), alginates/ hydrofibre (heavy exudate, bleeding), antimicrobial — silver, iodine, honey, PHMB (critical colonisation), non-adherent contact layers (paraffin gauze, silicone); change frequency by exudate", "Avoid sensitisers (lanolin, neomycin, fragrance) — ulcer patients have high contact allergy rates"]),
 ("Pyoderma gangrenosum as a cause of leg ulcer — clues.", ["Rapidly enlarging painful ulcer with violaceous undermined border, purulent base, starts as pustule, pathergy (worsens after debridement/ surgery), cribriform scarring; associations — IBD, arthritis, haematological malignancy (myeloma, leukaemia), PAPA", "Diagnosis of exclusion (PARACELSUS/ Delphi criteria); biopsy edge to exclude infection/ vasculitis/ malignancy; treat with systemic steroids, cyclosporine, TNF inhibitors (infliximab), IVIG, topical tacrolimus/ steroids; avoid surgical debridement"]),
 ("Haematological ulcers — which and why?", ["Sickle cell disease (malleolar ulcers — vaso-occlusion, haemolysis), thalassaemia, hereditary spherocytosis, polycythaemia vera, thrombocytosis, cryoglobulinaemia, cryofibrinogenaemia, antiphospholipid syndrome, protein C/S deficiency — painful ulcers, often around ankles; check CBC, peripheral smear, Hb electrophoresis, thrombophilia screen"]),
 ("What is Martorell's ulcer?", ["Hypertensive ischaemic leg ulcer — very painful necrotic ulcer on the lateral/ posterolateral distal leg (often over Achilles) in elderly with long-standing poorly controlled hypertension (and diabetes); arteriolosclerosis with medial calcification; pulses present; treat with BP control, debridement, split-skin grafting (best), pain control"]),
 ("Calciphylaxis — features.", ["Calcific uraemic arteriolopathy in end-stage renal disease (and non-uraemic — warfarin, hyperparathyroidism): extremely painful violaceous retiform purpura → necrotic ulcers on thighs, abdomen, legs; high mortality (sepsis); biopsy shows vascular calcification and thrombosis; treat with sodium thiosulphate IV, stop warfarin/ calcium/ vitamin D, cinacalcet/ parathyroidectomy, wound care, hyperbaric oxygen"]),
 ("Neuropathic ulcer in leprosy — management.", ["Plantar ulcer over metatarsal heads/ heel due to anaesthesia, dryness, autonomic loss, deformity; rest and offloading (no walking on ulcer), cleaning and dressing, treat infection, below-knee plaster cast for non-healing simple ulcers, protective MCR footwear with insoles, self-care (soak, scrape, oil), surgery for complicated ulcers (osteomyelitis — sequestrectomy)"]),
 ("What investigations confirm osteomyelitis under an ulcer?", ["Probe-to-bone test (positive predicts osteomyelitis in diabetic foot), plain X-ray (periosteal reaction, bone destruction — late), MRI (most sensitive), ESR/CRP, bone biopsy for culture and histology (gold standard)"]),
 ("Newer advanced therapies for chronic ulcers.", ["Negative pressure wound therapy (NPWT/ VAC), autologous platelet-rich plasma/fibrin, growth factors (becaplermin — PDGF), bioengineered skin substitutes (Apligraf, Dermagraft), amniotic membrane, epidermal grafting, hyperbaric oxygen (diabetic foot), electrical stimulation, larval (maggot) debridement therapy, ultrasound debridement"]),
 ("What lifestyle advice for venous ulcer patients?", ["Lifelong compression stockings, leg elevation above heart level when resting, regular walking and ankle exercises, weight reduction, avoid prolonged standing/ sitting, skin moisturisation, stop smoking, early review if skin changes or injury"]),
 ("Differential diagnosis of an ulcer with raised everted edge.", ["SCC (Marjolin's ulcer), BCC (rolled pearly edge), keratoacanthoma, melanoma (amelanotic), lymphoma, Kaposi sarcoma, deep fungal infection (chromoblastomycosis, sporotrichosis), tuberculosis (verrucous), leishmaniasis — biopsy the edge"]),
 ("Vasculitic ulcer — clues.", ["Multiple, painful, punched-out ulcers with surrounding palpable purpura, livedo racemosa, nodules; systemic symptoms; RA/ SLE/ PAN/ cryoglobulinaemia/ ANCA vasculitis; biopsy (deep, edge) + DIF; treat underlying vasculitis with immunosuppression"]),
 ("What is the role of systemic drugs in venous ulcer healing?", ["Pentoxifylline 400 mg three times daily (reduces leucocyte activation, improves microcirculation) — improves healing with or without compression; micronised purified flavonoid fraction (MPFF/ diosmin) — adjunct; aspirin — evidence weak; zinc only if deficient; antibiotics only for clinical infection"]),
 ("Prognosis and recurrence.", ["~50–70% of venous ulcers heal by 12–24 weeks with good compression; recurrence 30–70% in 1 year without stockings and venous surgery; poor prognostic factors — large (>10 cm²), long duration (>6 months), ABPI <0.8, immobility, obesity, deep vein reflux/ post-thrombotic syndrome"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Hidradenitis suppurativa", qa=[
 ("Explain the pathogenesis — follicular occlusion concept.", ["Primary event is infundibular hyperkeratosis and follicular occlusion → dilatation and rupture → release of keratin and bacteria into dermis → intense innate inflammation (IL-1β, TNF-α, IL-17, IL-23) → abscesses, sinus tracts (epithelialised tunnels), scarring", "Contributors: genetics (γ-secretase genes NCSTN, PSENEN, PSEN1 in familial), obesity, smoking (nicotine stimulates follicular plugging), mechanical friction, hormones (androgens, premenstrual flares), dysbiosis — apocrine glands are secondarily involved"]),
 ("Describe Hurley staging.", ["Stage I: single or multiple abscesses without sinus tracts or scarring", "Stage II: recurrent abscesses with sinus tracts and scarring, single or multiple widely separated lesions", "Stage III: diffuse or near-diffuse involvement or multiple interconnected tracts and abscesses across an entire area"]),
 ("What is the follicular occlusion tetrad?", ["Hidradenitis suppurativa, acne conglobata, dissecting cellulitis of the scalp (perifolliculitis capitis abscedens et suffodiens), pilonidal sinus — share follicular occlusion pathogenesis"]),
 ("What syndromes include HS?", ["PASH (pyoderma gangrenosum, acne, suppurative hidradenitis), PAPASH (with pyogenic arthritis), PsAPASH (psoriatic arthritis), SAPHO; associations — Crohn's disease, spondyloarthritis, Down syndrome, Dowling–Degos disease (PSENEN), metabolic syndrome, PCOS"]),
 ("Ultrasound in HS.", ["High-frequency ultrasound detects subclinical lesions, measures abscesses and tunnels (fluid collections, widened follicles, dermal pseudocysts), guides surgery and staging (SOS-HS score); colour Doppler shows activity"]),
 ("Why is HS often misdiagnosed and delayed?", ["Mistaken for recurrent boils/ abscesses and treated with repeated short antibiotics and incision–drainage; average diagnostic delay 7–10 years; clue — recurrence at the same flexural sites, double comedones, sterile cultures, tunnels"]),
 ("What is the role of antibiotics and their limitations?", ["Anti-inflammatory and anti-biofilm effects: tetracyclines (12 weeks) for mild–moderate, clindamycin–rifampicin combination (10–12 weeks) for moderate–severe, ertapenem IV (6 weeks) rescue for severe; high relapse after stopping; resistance concerns"]),
 ("Adalimumab and other biologics — details.", ["Adalimumab: 160 mg week 0, 80 mg week 2, then 40 mg weekly (or 80 mg every other week) from week 4 — HiSCR ~50–60%; evaluate at 12 weeks", "Secukinumab 300 mg every 2–4 weeks; bimekizumab (IL-17A/F) — high responses; infliximab off-label; screen TB/ HBV; combine with surgery for tunnels (biologics don't close fibrotic tracts)"]),
 ("What is deroofing and how is it done?", ["Under local anaesthesia, a probe is passed into the sinus tract, the roof is excised with scissors/ electrosurgery, the base (gelatinous granulation) curetted, and the wound left to heal by secondary intention — low recurrence, tissue-sparing, office procedure for Hurley II tunnels"]),
 ("Pain management and quality of life in HS.", ["HS has the worst DLQI impact among skin diseases; pain from nodules/ abscesses — NSAIDs, paracetamol, intralesional steroids (triamcinolone 10 mg/ml for acute nodules — quick relief), gabapentin/ pregabalin for neuropathic pain, avoid opioids long term; psychological support, depression/ suicide screening, sexual health"]),
 ("Lifestyle advice in HS.", ["Weight loss (bariatric surgery for morbid obesity improves HS), stop smoking, loose clothing, avoid shaving/ friction, antiseptic washes (chlorhexidine, benzoyl peroxide), zinc supplements, dairy/ brewer's yeast avoidance reported to help some"]),
 ("Histopathology in more detail.", ["Follicular occlusion with infundibular hyperkeratosis, ruptured follicles with mixed inflammation (neutrophils, lymphocytes, plasma cells, foreign-body giant cells), sinus tracts lined by squamous epithelium, dermal fibrosis; apocrine gland involvement secondary"]),
 ("HS in women — hormonal treatments.", ["Premenstrual flares, PCOS association; combined OCPs with anti-androgenic progestins, spironolactone 100–150 mg/day, finasteride 5 mg/day (post-menopausal or with contraception), metformin (insulin resistance) — useful adjuncts"]),
 ("Differential diagnosis (expanded).", ["Furunculosis/ carbuncles, infected epidermoid/ pilonidal cysts, Bartholin's abscess, Crohn's disease (perianal/ genital fistulae, metastatic Crohn's), scrofuloderma, actinomycosis, LGV, donovanosis, granuloma inguinale, cutaneous TB, deep fungal infection, Fox–Fordyce disease, SCC in chronic HS"]),
 ("Disease activity scoring — HS-PGA and Sartorius.", ["Hidradenitis Suppurativa Physician Global Assessment (6 levels: clear to very severe), Modified Sartorius score (counts nodules, fistulas, distance between lesions, Hurley stage per region)"]),
 ("Surgery after biologics — timing.", ["Combine biologics to quieten inflammation then excise/ deroof residual tunnels and scars; continue biologic perioperatively (no increase in infection in studies) to reduce recurrence"]),
 ("Paediatric HS — features.", ["Onset before puberty suggests premature adrenarche, obesity, endocrine disorders (precocious puberty, CAH); treat with topical clindamycin, antiseptics, weight loss, adalimumab approved ≥12 y; avoid tetracyclines <8 y"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Xeroderma pigmentosum", qa=[
 ("Explain nucleotide excision repair and why its defect causes skin cancer.", ["UV (mainly UVB) produces cyclobutane pyrimidine dimers and 6-4 photoproducts in DNA; NER recognises (XPC, XPE), unwinds (XPB, XPD — TFIIH), excises (XPG, XPF–ERCC1) the damaged segment and polymerase fills the gap", "Defect → unrepaired UV lesions → C→T and CC→TT 'UV signature' mutations in p53, PTCH1, oncogenes → early skin cancers; neurons (oxidative DNA damage repaired by NER) degenerate → neurological disease"]),
 ("Describe the cutaneous course of XP.", ["Severe sunburn on minimal exposure in ~50% (XPA, XPD) from infancy; others show early freckling (by age 2) without burning (XPC, XPE)", "Progressive photoageing: xerosis, poikiloderma (hypo/hyperpigmentation, telangiectasia, atrophy), actinic keratoses, keratoacanthomas, BCC, SCC, melanoma (lentigo maligna) in childhood on face, lips, tip of tongue"]),
 ("Ocular involvement.", ["Photophobia, conjunctivitis, keratitis, corneal opacification, ectropion, loss of eyelashes, conjunctival/ corneal SCC and melanoma of exposed eye; protection with UV-blocking glasses, lubricants"]),
 ("How is XP diagnosed?", ["Clinical features + cellular tests: unscheduled DNA synthesis (UDS) in cultured fibroblasts after UV, UV-sensitivity/ host-cell reactivation assays, recovery of RNA synthesis; molecular genetic testing (complementation group); prenatal diagnosis possible"]),
 ("Photoprotection measures in XP (practical).", ["Avoid all daylight UV — stay indoors in daytime, UV-blocking films on windows and car glass, UV meters, protective clothing, wide-brimmed hats with UV-protective face shields, gloves, high SPF broad-spectrum sunscreens reapplied, wrap-around UV sunglasses; 'children of the night' — schooling arrangements", "Vitamin D supplementation (deficiency due to sun avoidance)"]),
 ("Treatment of skin lesions in XP.", ["Frequent (3–6 monthly) full-skin examinations; early excision of suspicious lesions (Mohs for facial tumours), cryotherapy/ curettage for small lesions; field therapy for AKs — 5-FU, imiquimod, PDT (caution), chemical peels/ dermabrasion; oral retinoids (isotretinoin/ acitretin) for chemoprevention; nicotinamide; T4 endonuclease V liposome lotion (experimental); immunotherapy (PD-1 inhibitors) for advanced cancers — XP tumours respond well (high mutation burden); radiotherapy can be used when needed (repair of ionising-radiation damage is intact) but with caution"]),
 ("Differentiate XP from other photosensitive genodermatoses.", ["Cockayne syndrome (CSA/CSB — photosensitivity without increased cancer, cachectic dwarfism, 'bird-like' face, progeria, cataracts, retinal pigmentation, neurodegeneration)", "Trichothiodystrophy (XPD/TTDN1 — brittle sulphur-deficient hair with 'tiger-tail' banding on polarised light, ichthyosis, intellectual disability, photosensitivity, no cancer increase)", "Bloom syndrome (BLM — telangiectatic facial erythema, short stature, immunodeficiency, cancers — leukaemia), Rothmund–Thomson (RECQL4 — poikiloderma from infancy, skeletal defects, osteosarcoma), porphyrias (EPP — burning pain), Hartnup disease, Kindler syndrome"]),
 ("Genetic counselling in XP.", ["Autosomal recessive — 25% recurrence; high in consanguineous communities (India, Middle East, North Africa); carrier testing for siblings; prenatal diagnosis"]),
 ("Prognosis.", ["Without protection, median survival historically ~32 years (mainly metastatic skin cancer, neurological degeneration); strict photoprotection from early childhood markedly reduces cancers"]),
 ("What is the De Sanctis–Cacchione syndrome?", ["Severe XP (usually XPA) with microcephaly, progressive intellectual disability, dwarfism, hypogonadism, sensorineural deafness, spasticity/ ataxia — neurological XP"]),
 ("XP variant — how is it different?", ["Defect in DNA polymerase η (POLH) for translesion synthesis; NER normal; milder, later onset (2nd–4th decade) skin cancers, no neurological disease; cellular test — UV sensitivity in presence of caffeine"]),
 ("Role of dermatology team in school and social life.", ["Educate teachers/ family, organise indoor activities, UV-safe classrooms (film on windows, LED lighting low-UV), psychological support, support groups ('XP Family Support')"]),
 ("Internal and other cancers in XP.", ["~10–20 fold increased internal cancers — CNS tumours (gliomas), lung (smokers), haematological (leukaemia), thyroid; avoid smoking and other carcinogens"]),
 ("What anaesthesia/ procedural precautions?", ["Light sources in operating theatres and surgical lamps can emit UV — use filters/ LED; minimise fluorescent exposure; ophthalmic procedures need UV protection"]),
 ("Why are lip, tongue tip and eyes common tumour sites?", ["Highly sun-exposed mucosal sites without melanin protection and with frequent UV exposure (lower lip, anterior tongue, conjunctiva/ cornea) — SCC of tongue tip is almost exclusive to XP in children"]),
 ("Chemopreventive retinoid regimen.", ["Isotretinoin 0.5–2 mg/kg/day reduced new skin cancers by ~60% in XP (Kraemer study) but with significant toxicity; lower doses (0.5 mg/kg) or acitretin used; tumours recur on stopping — long-term use with monitoring"]),
 ]),
]
